2\\times10 ^ 5\n- 1\\leq M\\leq2\\times10 ^ 5\n- 1\\leq l _ i,d _ i,k _ i,c _ i\\leq10 ^ 9\\ (1\\leq i\\leq M)\n- 1\\leq A _ i,B _ i\\leq N\\ (1\\leq i\\leq M)\n- A _ i\\neq B _ i\\ (1\\leq i\\leq M)\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 7\n10 5 10 3 1 3\n13 5 10 2 3 4\n15 5 10 7 4 6\n3 10 2 4 2 5\n7 10 2 3 5 6\n5 3 18 2 2 3\n6 3 20 4 2 1\n\nSample Output 1\n\n55\n56\n58\n60\n17\n\nThe following diagram shows the trains running in the country (information about arrival and departure times is omitted).\n\nConsider the latest time at which one can arrive at station 6 from station 2.\nAs shown in the following diagram, one can arrive at station 6 by departing from station 2 at time 56 and moving as station 2\\rightarrow station 3\\rightarrow station 4\\rightarrow station 6.\n\nIt is impossible to depart from station 2 after time 56 and arrive at station 6, so f(2)=56.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n5 5\n1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1000000000 1 5\n5 9 2 6 2 3\n10 4 1 6 2 3\n1 1 1 1 3 5\n3 1 4 1 5 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n1000000000000000000\nUnreachable\n1\nUnreachable\n\nThere is a train that departs from station 1 at time 10 ^ {{18}} and arrives at station 5 at time 10 ^ {{18}}+10 ^ 9. There are no trains departing from station 1 after that time, so f(1)=10 ^ {{18}}.\nAs seen here, the answer may not fit within a 32\\operatorname{{bit}} integer.\nAlso, both the second and third pieces of information guarantee that there is a train that departs from station 2 at time 14 and arrives at station 3 at time 20.\nAs seen here, some trains may appear in multiple pieces of information.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n16 20\n4018 9698 2850 3026 8 11\n2310 7571 7732 1862 13 14\n2440 2121 20 1849 11 16\n2560 5115 190 3655 5 16\n1936 6664 39 8822 4 16\n7597 8325 20 7576 12 5\n5396 1088 540 7765 15 1\n3226 88 6988 2504 13 5\n1838 7490 63 4098 8 3\n1456 5042 4 2815 14 7\n3762 6803 5054 6994 10 9\n9526 6001 61 8025 7 8\n5176 6747 107 3403 1 5\n2014 5533 2031 8127 8 11\n8102 5878 58 9548 9 10\n3788 174 3088 5950 3 13\n7778 5389 100 9003 10 15\n556 9425 9458 109 3 11\n5725 7937 10 3282 2 9\n6951 7211 8590 1994 15 12\n\nSample Output 3\n\n720358\n77158\n540926\n255168\n969295\nUnreachable\n369586\n466218\n343148\n541289\n42739\n165772\n618082\n16582\n591828",
    "prediction": "claude-opus-4-7",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "<think>\nReverse graph. For each edge info (l,d,k,c,A,B): trains depart A at times l, l+d,..., l+(k-1)d, arrive B at those+c. We want max arrival time at N starting from S.\n\nDefine g(v) = f(v) but for reverse: g(N) = infinity essentially. Actually f(v) = latest departure time from v to reach N.\n\nFor edge A->B with departure time t, arrival t+c. To use this edge, we need t+c <= f(B) (if B != N), meaning we can then continue from B. If B = N, no constraint (any t works).\n\nSo for edge A->B: max t in {l, l+d,..., l+(k-1)d} such that t+c <= f(B). Then this contributes candidate for f(A) = t.\n\nWait, f(A) is max over edges leaving A. For each edge, max valid t.\n\nIf B = N: max t = l+(k-1)d, so contributes l+(k-1)d to f(A).\nElse: need t <= f(B)-c. Max t in arithmetic sequence <= f(B)-c. If l > f(B)-c, no valid. Else t_max = l + min(k-1, (f(B)-c-l)//d) * d.\n\nThis is like a DAG relaxation but graph may have cycles. Use Dijkstra-like approach with max-heap: process nodes in decreasing order of f value. Start with f(N) = infinity (or treat specially).\n\nActually, once we know f(B), we can compute contribution to f(A) via each edge into A (reverse). Build reverse adjacency: for each edge info, store it indexed by B (destination). When we finalize f(B), iterate all edges with destination B, compute candidate for f(A).\n\nUse max-heap. Initialize: for edges with B=N, for each such edge, candidate for A is l+(k-1)d. Push (candidate, A). Then process max-heap: pop node with largest f, if not finalized, finalize, then look at all edges where this node is B, compute candidate for A.\n\nWait actually N itself: f(N) undefined (we're asked f(1)..f(N-1)). But we handle B=N specially.\n\nLet me structure: group edges by B. For each B, list of (l,d,k,c,A).\n\nheap = max-heap of (candidate_f_value, node).\nFor each edge with B=N: push (l+(k-1)d, A).\n\nThen pop max: (val, u). If u already finalized, skip. Else f[u] = val. For each edge with B=u: compute max t with t+c <= val, t in {l,...,l+(k-1)d}. If exists, push (t, A).\n\nThis is like Dijkstra with max priority. Complexity O((N+M) log). Each edge processed each time its B is finalized — once. So total O(M log N).\n\nLet me verify: each B finalized once, edges grouped by B processed once. Good.\n\nEdge case: multiple edges info entries with same B — all handled in the list.\n\nLet me code.\n</think>\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport heapq\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()\n    idx = 0\n    N = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n    M = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n    \n    edges_by_B = defaultdict(list)  # B -> list of (l,d,k,c,A)\n    initial = []  # candidates for edges to N\n    \n    for _ in range(M):\n        l = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n        d = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n        k = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n        c = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n        A = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n        B = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n        if B == N:\n            initial.append((A, l + (k-1)*d))\n        edges_by_B[B].append((l,d,k,c,A))\n    \n    f = [None] * (N+1)\n    # max-heap via negation\n    heap = []\n    for A, val in initial:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-val, A))\n    \n    while heap:\n        neg_val, u = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        val = -neg_val\n        if f[u] is not None:\n            continue\n        f[u] = val\n        # process edges with B = u\n        for (l,d,k,c,A) in edges_by_B[u]:\n            limit = val - c\n            if l > limit:\n                continue\n            steps = (limit - l) // d\n            if steps > k-1:\n                steps = k-1\n            t = l + steps * d\n            if f[A] is None or f[A] < t:\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-t, A))\n    \n    out = []\n    for i in range(1, N):\n        if f[i] is None:\n            out.append(\"Unreachable\")\n        else:\n            out.append(str(f[i]))\n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(out) + \"\\n\")\n\nmain()\n`