      "generated_answer": "<think>\nDP with two states: caps off/on.\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    import sys\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    X, Y, Z = int(input_data[0]), int(input_data[1]), int(input_data[2])\n    S = input_data[3]\n    INF = float('inf')\n    # dp[0] = caps off, dp[1] = caps on\n    dp = [0, Z]  # initially off; to be on costs Z\n    # Actually start: dp[0]=0, dp[1]=INF initially, but we can toggle anytime\n    # Better: dp[0]=0, dp[1]=Z (cost to reach on state with empty string)\n    for c in S:\n        new = [INF, INF]\n        # from off state\n        # produce c while off:\n        if c == 'a':\n            cost_off = X  # stay off\n            cost_on = Y   # stay on (shift+a gives a when on)\n        else:  # 'A'\n            cost_off = Y\n            cost_on = X\n        # transitions: end state can be off or on regardless (toggle before or after)\n        # from dp[0] (off), to produce char and end off: cost_off, or toggle twice = cost_off+2Z, or... \n        # Actually simpler: for each prev state s, for each next state t, cost = min over ways\n        # ways: toggle 0 or 1 time before pressing, and possibly toggle after? But toggling after is same as toggling at start of next char. So just consider toggle before.\n        # If prev=s, we may toggle to s' (cost 0 if s'==s else Z), then press key. Press key in state s' produces c with cost X if (s'==off and c=='a') or (s'==on and c=='A'), else Y. End state = s'.\n        for s in range(2):\n            for sp in range(2):\n                toggle = 0 if s == sp else Z\n                if (sp == 0 and c == 'a') or (sp == 1 and c == 'A'):\n                    press = X\n                else:\n                    press = Y\n                total = dp[s] + toggle + press\n                if total < new[sp]:\n                    new[sp] = total\n        dp = new\n    print(min(dp[0], dp[1]))\n\nmain()\n```",
