    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_243",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nWe have N dice.\r\nFor each i = 1, 2, \\ldots, N, when the i-th die is thrown, it shows a random integer between 1 and A_i, inclusive, with equal probability.\nFind the probability, modulo 998244353, that the following condition is satisfied when the N dice are thrown simultaneously.\n\nThere is a way to choose some (possibly all) of the N dice so that the sum of their results is 10.\n\n How to find a probability modulo 998244353\nIt can be proved that the sought probability is always a rational number. Additionally, the constraints of this problem guarantee that if the sought probability is represented as an irreducible fraction \\frac{{y}}{{x}}, then x is not divisible by 998244353. Here, there is a unique integer z such that xz \\equiv y \\pmod{{998244353}}. Report this z.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\r\nA_1 A_2 \\ldots A_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1 \\leq N \\leq 100\n- 1 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^6\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4\r\n1 7 2 9\n\nSample Output 1\n\n942786334\r\n\nFor instance, if the first, second, third, and fourth dice show 1, 3, 2, and 7, respectively, these results satisfy the condition.\r\nIn fact, if the second and fourth dice are chosen, the sum of their results is 3 + 7 = 10.\r\nAlternatively, if the first, third, and fourth dice are chosen, the sum of their results is 1 + 2 + 7 = 10.\nOn the other hand, if the first, second, third, and fourth dice show 1, 6, 1, and 5, respectively, there is no way to choose some of them so that the sum of their results is 10, so the condition is not satisfied.\nIn this sample input, the probability of the results of the N dice satisfying the condition is \\frac{{11}}{{18}}.\r\nThus, print this value modulo 998244353, that is, 942786334.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n7\r\n1 10 100 1000 10000 100000 1000000\n\nSample Output 2\n\n996117877",
    "prediction": "claude-opus-4-7",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "<think>\nWe need probability that some subset sums to 10. Each die i shows value v_i in [1,A_i]. We want P(exists subset S with sum_{i in S} v_i = 10).\n\nDP: process dice one by one, track which subset sums in {0,...,10} are achievable. State = bitmask of size 11 (bits 0..10). We want probability that bit 10 is set at the end.\n\nInitial state: {0} = mask with bit 0 set = 1.\n\nWhen adding die with value v (1..A_i), new achievable set = old_set ∪ (old_set + v), keeping only values ≤10.\n\nFor each die, the value v is uniform over 1..A_i. We need to compute transition: for each old mask m, and each v in 1..A_i, compute new mask m'. But A_i can be up to 10^6.\n\nKey: only v in 1..10 actually matter differently. If v > 10, then old_set + v gives only values >10, so new_set = old_set (since 0+v>10 too... wait, if 0 is in old_set and v>10, then 0+v>10, discarded). So for v ≥ 11, new mask = old mask.\n\nWait, but what if old set has some element x with x+v ≤ 10? That requires x ≤ 10-v. If v ≥ 11, no such x (x≥0). So yes, v ≥ 11 leaves mask unchanged.\n\nSo for die i with A_i values:\n- For v=1..min(10,A_i): transition mask m -> m | (m shifted by v, truncated to 11 bits)\n- For v=11..A_i (if A_i ≥ 11): mask unchanged. Count = max(0, A_i - 10).\n\nEach with probability 1/A_i.\n\nSo DP: prob[mask] for each of 2^11 = 2048 masks. For each die, compute new distribution.\n\nFor each die i:\n- Compute p_stay = max(0, A_i - 10) / A_i, plus for v=1..min(10,A_i), each has prob 1/A_i.\n\nActually for each old mask m, we compute contribution to each new mask. Let's iterate:\n\nnew_prob[m'] += prob[m] * (count of v giving m -> m') / A_i\n\nFor each mask m (must contain bit 0), for each v in 1..min(10, A_i), compute m' = (m | (m << v)) & ((1<<11)-1). Also handle v>10 case.\n\nComplexity: 100 dice * 2048 masks * 10 = ~2M ops. Fine.\n\nAlso we should only consider masks containing bit 0 (always 1). Actually mask always has bit 0 since we start with 0. Shifting preserves this? m | (m<<v): bit 0 comes from m, still there. Yes.\n\nSo masks always have bit 0. So effectively 1024 states.\n\nLet me code:\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef solve():\n    N, A = read\n    inv = modular inverse\n    prob = dict, prob[1] = 1  (mask with only bit 0)\n    for a in A:\n        inv_a = pow(a, MOD-2, MOD)\n        new_prob = defaultdict(int)\n        # count of v>10: max(0, a-10)\n        stay_count = max(0, a-10)\n        vmax = min(10, a)\n        for m, p in prob.items():\n            if stay_count:\n                new_prob[m] = (new_prob[m] + p * stay_count) % MOD\n            # but we need to multiply by inv_a at the end\n            for v in range(1, vmax+1):\n                mp = (m | (m << v)) & ((1<<11)-1)\n                new_prob[mp] = (new_prob[mp] + p) % MOD\n        # multiply all by inv_a\n        prob = {m: (p * inv_a) % MOD for m, p in new_prob.items()}\n    ans = 0\n    for m, p in prob.items():\n        if m & (1<<10):\n            ans = (ans + p) % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\nLooks good.\n</think>\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\ndef solve():\n    MOD = 998244353\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0])\n    A = [int(x) for x in data[1:1+N]]\n    \n    MASK = (1 << 11) - 1\n    prob = {1: 1}  # only bit 0 set (sum 0 achievable)\n    \n    for a in A:\n        inv_a = pow(a, MOD - 2, MOD)\n        new_prob = defaultdict(int)\n        stay_count = max(0, a - 10)\n        vmax = min(10, a)\n        for m, p in prob.items():\n            if stay_count:\n                new_prob[m] = (new_prob[m] + p * stay_count) % MOD\n            for v in range(1, vmax + 1):\n                mp = (m | (m << v)) & MASK\n                new_prob[mp] = (new_prob[mp] + p) % MOD\n        prob = {m: (p * inv_a) % MOD for m, p in new_prob.items()}\n    \n    ans = 0\n    for m, p in prob.items():\n        if m & (1 << 10):\n            ans = (ans + p) % MOD\n    print(ans)\n\nsolve()\n```",
