    "prediction": "claude-opus-4-7",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "<think>\nFor each subarray, imbalance = (number of pairs (a,b) in sorted where b-a>1). Equivalent: for each element added, count contribution.\n\nStandard approach: For each index i, count number of subarrays where nums[i] contributes to imbalance. Consider each pair... Actually the trick: imbalance count in subarray = number of j in subarray such that (j+1) not in subarray but there exists element < j in subarray... hmm.\n\nAlternative: imbalance = sum over values v in subarray of [v is \"start of gap\"], meaning v is in subarray but v+1 is not, and v is not the max of subarray.\n\nSo for each index i with value v = nums[i], count subarrays containing i where:\n- no index in subarray has value v+1\n- there exists index in subarray with value > v (so i is not max)\n\nTo avoid double counting when multiple indices have same value v: pick the leftmost occurrence of v in subarray to represent it (or rightmost).\n\nStandard: for each i, count subarrays where nums[i] contributes. Let's say i contributes if it's the \"leftmost occurrence of value v\" in subarray AND v+1 doesn't appear AND max > v.\n\nLet me think differently: total imbalance = sum over subarrays of (distinct sorted gaps). \n\nKnown solution: For each i, find:\n- left = nearest index j < i with nums[j] == nums[i] or nums[j] == nums[i]-1 (leftmost that we can't cross)\n- right = nearest index j > i with nums[j] == nums[i]-1\n\nWait, let me look up mentally. The contribution: for element nums[i], count as gap-start (meaning nums[i] is in sorted, nums[i]+1 is not... no wait).\n\nLet me redefine. In sorted subarray, gap at position i means sarr[i+1] - sarr[i] > 1. So the pair (sarr[i], sarr[i+1]) with difference > 1. Equivalently, for value v = sarr[i], v is in subarray, v+1 is not in subarray, and there's some value > v+1 in subarray (i.e., v is not the max).\n\nFor multi-occurrences of v, only one gap is counted. So for each index i with value v, count it if it's the *first* occurrence (leftmost) of v in the subarray, v+1 doesn't appear, and max > v.\n\nSo for index i with v = nums[i]:\n- Find L: largest j < i such that nums[j] == v (can't have another v to the left in subarray). So subarray start > L.\n- Also can't have nums[j] == v+1 in subarray... wait no, v+1 must NOT be in subarray at all.\n- Find nearest j < i with nums[j] == v+1: call it L1. Subarray start > L1.\n- Actually left boundary: start must be > max(L, L1)? No wait, we need no v+1 anywhere in subarray, and no earlier v.\n\nHmm let me reconsider. Left boundary: subarray [l, r] with l <= i <= r.\n- Need: no j in [l, i-1] with nums[j] == v (so i is leftmost v). So l > (last v before i).\n- Need: no j in [l, r] with nums[j] == v+1.\n- Need: exists j in [l, r] with nums[j] > v.\n\nLet leftV = last index < i with nums[j] == v, else -1.\nFor v+1 constraint, both sides matter.\n\nThis is getting complex. Given n ≤ 1000, O(n^2) works.\n\nSimple O(n^2): for each starting index l, maintain sorted structure as r increases, track imbalance count. Adding one element: use a sorted set, when inserting x, check neighbors. If left neighbor lo and right neighbor hi exist:\n- If hi - lo > 1 previously contributed 1 to imbalance.\n- After inserting x: gap(lo,x) contributes if x-lo>1, gap(x,hi) if hi-x>1.\n- Delta = (x-lo>1) + (hi-x>1) - (hi-lo>1).\n\nIf only left neighbor: delta = (x-lo>1).\nIf only right neighbor: delta = (hi-x>1).\nIf none: delta = 0.\n\nUse SortedList. n=1000 gives 10^6 ops, fine.\n\nLet me code with sortedcontainers.\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef sumImbalanceNumbers(nums):\n    from sortedcontainers import SortedList\n    n = len(nums)\n    total = 0\n    for l in range(n):\n        sl = SortedList()\n        imb = 0\n        for r in range(l, n):\n            x = nums[r]\n            if x in sl:\n                # duplicate, no change\n                sl.add(x)\n            else:\n                idx = sl.bisect_left(x)\n                has_left = idx > 0\n                has_right = idx < len(sl)\n                if has_left and has_right:\n                    lo = sl[idx-1]\n                    hi = sl[idx]\n                    delta = (1 if x-lo>1 else 0) + (1 if hi-x>1 else 0) - (1 if hi-lo>1 else 0)\n                    imb += delta\n                elif has_left:\n                    lo = sl[idx-1]\n                    if x-lo>1: imb += 1\n                elif has_right:\n                    hi = sl[idx]\n                    if hi-x>1: imb += 1\n                sl.add(x)\n            total += imb\n    return total\n```",
