st code the spiral like the sample.\n\nSpiral from outside in, starting at (1,1) going right:\n- direction: right, down, left, up\n- shrink boundaries\n\nLet me code the spiral:\n\n```python\nn = int(input())\ngrid = [[0]*n for _ in range(n)]\nc = n // 2  # center index (0-based)\nval = 1\ntop, bottom, left, right = 0, n-1, 0, n-1\ndr = 0\nwhile top <= bottom and left <= right:\n    # go right along top\n    for j in range(left, right+1):\n        grid[top][j] = val; val += 1\n    top += 1\n    # go down along right\n    for i in range(top, bottom+1):\n        grid[i][right] = val; val += 1\n    right -= 1\n    # go left along bottom\n    if top <= bottom:\n        for j in range(right, left-1, -1):\n            grid[bottom][j] = val; val += 1\n        bottom -= 1\n    # go up along left\n    if left <= right:\n        for i in range(bottom, top-1, -1):\n            grid[i][left] = val; val += 1\n        left += 1\n```\n\nThis fills 1..N² in spiral. The center gets N². We need T at center, but also numbers must be consecutive with center skipped. In spiral, center is the last cell (val=N²), so parts 1..N²-1 fill everything else and center is T. \n\nWait but the problem says parts 1 to N²-1, so we have N²-1 parts. Spiral fills N² cells with values 1..N². The center gets value N². Replace it with T. Parts 1..N²-1 are placed and consecutive as needed since spiral is a path.\n\nLet me verify with sample N=5. Sample output 1 is exactly the spiral! Great.\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    n = int(input())\n    grid = [[0]*n for _ in range(n)]\n    val = 1\n    top, bottom, left, right = 0, n-1, 0, n-1\n    while top <= bottom and left <= right:\n        for j in range(left, right+1):\n            grid[top][j] = val; val += 1\n        top += 1\n        for i in range(top, bottom+1):\n            grid[i][right] = val; val += 1\n        right -= 1\n        if top <= bottom:\n            for j in range(right, left-1, -1):\n                grid[bottom][j] = val; val +=