=0, so 0\n    best_prev = [0] * n  # for j=0, best is 0\n    \n    for j in range(1, k+1):\n        m = (k - j + 1) * (1 if j % 2 == 1 else -1)\n        take = [NEG] * n\n        best = [NEG] * n\n        for i in range(n):\n            # take[j][i] = m*nums[i] + max(take[i-1], best_prev[i-1])\n            prev_take = take[i-1] if i > 0 else NEG\n            prev_best = best_prev[i-1] if i > 0 else 0  # for j=1, best[0][-1]=0\n            # Actually for j>1, best_prev[-1] should be NEG (can't have j-1 subarrays in empty)\n            if i == 0:\n                prev_best = 0 if j == 1 else NEG\n            else:\n                prev_best = best_prev[i-1]\n            \n            best_option = max(prev_take, prev_best) if prev_take != NEG or prev_best != NEG else NEG\n            if best_option == NEG:\n                take[i] = NEG\n            else:\n                take[i] = m * nums[i] + best_option\n            \n            best[i] = max(best[i-1] if i > 0 else NEG, take[i])\n        \n        best_prev = best\n    \n    return best_prev[n-1]\n```\n\nWait, for j=1, i=0: prev_take = NEG, prev_best = 0, so take[0] = m*nums[0]. Good.\n\nFor j≥2, i=0: can't fit j subarrays in 1 element, so take[0] = NEG. prev_best = NEG (best_prev for j-1 at i=-1 doesn't exist). Good.\n\nBut wait, best_prev is best[j-1][i], the max take[j-1][p] for p≤i. For j-1=0, this should be 0 everywhere. I initialized to [0]*n. Good.\n\nLet me verify with example 1: nums=[1,2,3,-1,2], k=3.\nm_1 = 3, m_2 = -2, m_3 = 1.\n\nj=1, m=3:\ni=0: take[0] = 3*1 = 3, best[0]=3\ni=1: max(take[0]=3, best_prev[0]=0)=3, take[1]=3+3*2=9, best[1]=9\ni=2: max(9, 0)=9, take[2]=9+3*3=18, best[2]=18\ni=3: max(18, 0)=18, take[3]=18+3*(-1)=15, best[3]=18\ni=4: max(15, 0)=15... wait max(take[3]=15, best_prev[3]=0)=15, take[4]=15+3*2=21, best[4]=21\n\nj=2, m=-2:\ni=0: take[0]=NEG (since j=2, i=0, prev_best=NEG)\ni=1: prev_take=NEG, prev_best=best_prev[0]=3, take[1]=3+(-2)*2=-1, best[1]=-1\ni=2: prev_take=-1, prev_best=9, max=9, take[2]=9+(-2)*3=3, best[2]=3\ni=3: prev_take=3, prev_best=18, max=18, take[3]=18+(-2)*(-1)=20, best[3]=20\ni=4: prev_take=20, prev_best=18, max=20, take[4]=20+(-2)*2=16, best[4]=20\n\nj=3, m=1:\ni=0: NEG\ni=1: NEG (prev_best=best_prev[0] for j=2 which is NEG)\ni=2: prev_take=NEG, prev_best=best_prev[1]=-1, take[2]=-1+1*3=2, best[2]=2\ni=3: prev_take=2, prev_best=3, max=3, take[3]=3+1*(-1)=2, best[3]=2\ni=4: prev_take=2, prev_best=20, max=20, take[4]=20+1*2=22, best[4]=22\n\nAnswer 22. Correct!\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef maximumStrength(nums, k):\n    n = len(nums)\n    NEG = float('-inf')\n    best_prev = [0] * n  # for j=0, best is 0 everywhere\n    \n    for j in range(1, k + 1):\n        m = (k - j + 1) * (1 if j % 2 == 1 else -1)\n        take = [NEG] * n\n        best = [NEG] * n\n        for i in range(n):\n            prev_take = take[i - 1] if i > 0 else NEG\n            if i == 0:\n                prev_best = 0 if j == 1 else NEG\n            else:\n                prev_best = best_prev[i - 1]\n            \n            best_option = max(prev_take, prev_best)\n            if best_option == NEG:\n                take[i] = NEG\n            else:\n                take[i] = m * nums[i] + best_option\n            \n            best[i] = max(best[i - 1] if i > 0 else NEG, take[i])\n        \n        best_prev = best\n    \n    return best_prev[n - 1]\n```",
