    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "<think>\nGreedy: sort coupons by discount desc. For each coupon, assign to cheapest item with price >= L_i that's still available. Use sorted items and a data structure.\n\nActually standard approach: sort coupons by D descending. For each coupon, find item with price >= L_i, smallest such price (to save it for bigger... wait). We want to maximize total discount. Each coupon gives fixed discount D_i if applied to item with P >= L_i. So we want to apply as many coupons as possible, prioritizing high D_i.\n\nSort coupons by D desc. For each, pick any available item with P >= L_i. To leave flexibility, pick the smallest such P (since larger P items can satisfy more coupons). Use sorted multiset.\n\nUse sortedcontainers SortedList. For each coupon in order, bisect_left for L_i, pick that item, remove it. If no such item, skip.\n</think>\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom sortedcontainers import SortedList\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.buffer.read().split()\n    idx = 0\n    N = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n    M = int(data[idx]); idx+=1\n    P = [int(data[idx+i]) for i in range(N)]; idx+=N\n    L = [int(data[idx+i]) for i in range(M)]; idx+=M\n    D = [int(data[idx+i]) for i in range(M)]; idx+=M\n    \n    total = sum(P)\n    coupons = sorted(zip(D, L), reverse=True)\n    sl = SortedList(P)\n    discount = 0\n    for d, l in coupons:\n        pos = sl.bisect_left(l)\n        if pos < len(sl):\n            discount += d\n            del sl[pos]\n    print(total - discount)\n\nmain()\n```",
