    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "<think>\nDijkstra with two states: 0=car, 1=train. From car can switch to train at city (free). Actually simpler: compute shortest by car from 1 to i, then shortest by train from i to N, minimize sum. Since once switched can't go back, and car edges use full graph...\n\nActually car distances: Dijkstra on car graph from node 1. Train distances: Dijkstra on train graph from node N. Answer = min over i of car_dist[i] + train_dist[i].\n</think>\n\n```python\nimport heapq\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    idx = 0\n    N = int(input_data[idx]); idx+=1\n    A = int(input_data[idx]); idx+=1\n    B = int(input_data[idx]); idx+=1\n    C = int(input_data[idx]); idx+=1\n    D = []\n    for i in range(N):\n        row = [int(input_data[idx+j]) for j in range(N)]\n        idx += N\n        D.append(row)\n    \n    INF = float('inf')\n    \n    def dijkstra(src, mult, add):\n        dist = [INF]*N\n        dist[src] = 0\n        pq = [(0, src)]\n        while pq:\n            d, u = heapq.heappop(pq)\n            if d > dist[u]: continue\n            for v in range(N):\n                if v == u: continue\n                w = D[u][v]*mult + add\n                nd = d + w\n                if nd < dist[v]:\n                    dist[v] = nd\n                    heapq.heappush(pq, (nd, v))\n        return dist\n    \n    car = dijkstra(0, A, 0)\n    train = dijkstra(N-1, B, C)\n    \n    ans = min(car[i] + train[i] for i in range(N))\n    print(ans)\n\nmain()\n```",
