lip approach. For x-approach, any pairing works. So consecutive pairing is fine.\n\ndp[i] = min(dp[i-2] + min(x, d[i-1]-d[i-2]))... only choice. Let me verify example: s1=1100011000, s2=0101001010. Diffs at positions where they differ:\ns1: 1,1,0,0,0,1,1,0,0,0\ns2: 0,1,0,1,0,0,1,0,1,0\ndiff: 1,0,0,1,0,1,0,0,1,0 → positions 0,3,5,8. 4 diffs.\nPairs (0,3) and (5,8): min(2,3)+min(2,3)=2+2=4. ✓\n\nExample 2: diffs count odd? s1=10110, s2=00011. diff: 1,0,1,0,1 → 3 diffs, odd, return -1. ✓\n\nGreat, simple DP.\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef minOperations(s1, s2, x):\n    diffs = [i for i in range(len(s1)) if s1[i] != s2[i]]\n    if len(diffs) % 2 == 1:\n        return -1\n    n = len(diffs)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[2] = min(x, diffs[1] - diffs[0])\n    for i in range(4, n + 1, 2):\n        dp[i] = dp[i - 2] + min(x, diffs[i - 1] - diffs[i - 2])\n    return dp[n]\n```",
