      "generated_answer": "<think>\nLet me think about this problem.\n\nWe need to compute for each position, if we change s[i] to some char c, what's the max partitions.\n\nStandard approach: precompute from left, the partition count and mask state after processing prefix ending at each position, and from right, count of partitions if we start fresh at position i.\n\nLet me define:\n- For each i, `right[i]` = number of partitions when applying the greedy from position i to end (no changes).\n- For each i, we track the state going forward: (partitions_so_far, current_mask) after processing s[0..i-1] with greedy.\n\nActually let's think differently. Let's precompute:\n- prefix[i] = (partitions, mask, start_of_current_partition) — going greedy from left, after considering first i characters.\n\nWait, greedy: we take the longest prefix with ≤k distinct. When we hit a char that would make it k+1 distinct, we cut BEFORE that char and start new partition with that char.\n\nSo the state after position i: we have some current partition being built with a mask. When we add s[i]:\n- If s[i] in mask or popcount(mask) < k: add to current partition, mask |= bit\n- Else: cut here, partitions++, start new partition with just s[i], mask = bit\n\nLet prefixParts[i] = partitions completed before position i (i.e., cuts made when processing s[0..i-1]).\nLet prefixMask[i] = current mask at position i (before processing s[i]).\n\nSimilarly from right: suffixParts[i] = number of partitions when applying greedy on s[i..n-1] from scratch.\n\nFor change at position i to char c:\n- Simulate from position where change starts. But the change might merge with previous partition or split it.\n\nActually, the standard technique: iterate over i and c. Before position i, we have state (parts, mask) = (prefixParts[i], prefixMask[i]). Then we process s[i] as c: update state. Then we need to continue greedy from i+1 with this state until we reach a point where the state \"syncs\" with the natural right computation.\n\nBut we can't easily precompute all suffix states. However, we can just simulate forward from position i for each (i, c) pair — that's O(n * 26 * n) = O(n^2 * 26) which for n=10^4 is 2.6 * 10^9, too slow.\n\nBetter: for each i, we simulate from i but only need to go until we've made the cut after the changed character, then we can use suffix info.\n\nHmm, let me think. After changing s[i] to c and processing greedy from start:\n- We reach position i with state (p, m).\n- Process c: if c in m or |m|<k, new state (p, m|bit_c). Else, cut: (p+1, bit_c).\n- Continue processing s[i+1], s[i+2], ...\n\nThe continuation from i+1 depends on the mask at that point. Once we make the next cut at some position j > i, from j onwards, the greedy is identical to starting fresh at j, giving suffixParts[j] more partitions.\n\nSo we need: starting from state (mask') at position i+1 (where mask' is the mask after processing changed char), simulate greedy until the next cut happens at some position j, then answer = current_parts + 1 + suffixParts[j] (if j <= n-1), or just current_parts if we reach end.\n\nBut simulating from i+1 could take O(n) each time. Total O(n^2 * 26). For n=10^4, still 2.6e9.\n\nWait n=10^4, so n^2 = 10^8, times 26 = 2.6e9. Too slow in Python.\n\nHmm. Let me think of a smarter approach.\n\nAlternative: We can precompute for each position i and each mask m (with popcount ≤ k), the next cut position. But 2^26 masks... no.\n\nBetter observation: at any point during greedy, we're extending until we'd add a (k+1)th distinct char. So from position i with current mask having exactly k distinct chars, the next cut is at first position j >= i where s[j] not in mask. If mask has < k distinct, then... we'd add anything until we get k+1 distinct.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me think again.\n\nActually, let's think: the change at position i affects only if we change s[i]. Let's iterate through each i from 0 to n-1.\n\nFor each i, current prefix state is