ced cut)\n\nFor the simulation from i+1 with initial mask M, we want to know: (number of additional partitions).\n\nObservation: if M has popcount k, next cut is at first j >= i+1 with s[j] not in M.\nIf M has popcount < k, then... we extend until first j >= i+1 where s[j] makes popcount k+1, meaning M grows.\n\nPrecompute nextDiff[i][c] = smallest j >= i with s[j] == c? Or something.\n\nHmm, let me think differently. Precompute the natural greedy partition boundaries: positions p_0=0, p_1, p_2, ..., p_m where each partition is [p_i, p_{i+1}). And also right[i] = greedy partitions starting fresh from i.\n\nNow when we change s[i] to c and process greedy:\n- Before i: prefix processing gives state (parts_left, mask_left).\n- At i with char c: figure out new state.\n- After i: simulate until we \"resync\" with the natural partitioning.\n\nActually, key insight: from position i+1 onwards, if the current mask matches what natural greedy would have at some sync point, we can jump to using right values.\n\nLet me just code O(n^2) simulation without the 26. For each i, we compute the optimal c smartly:\n\nGiven state (p, m) before i, what's the best c?\n- Option A: c ∈ m. Then state after i is (p, m). Continue processing s[i+1..] with mask m: this is essentially like s[i] didn't exist. But wait, we still processed s[i] as c which is in m, so mask stays m. The subsequent processing depends on m and future chars.\n- Option B: c ∉ m, |m| < k. State after i: (p, m ∪ {c}). Bigger mask, may cut earlier later.\n- Option C: c ∉ m, |m| == k. Cut: state (p+1, {c}). This forces an extra partition here.\n\nFor option C, we can pick c to maximize future partitions. c should not be s[i+1]'s continuation.\n\nHmm, still complex.\n\nLet me just try the O(n^2) approach with small constant and see. n=10^4, so n^2 = 10^8. In Python this might be too slow but let's think of speedups.\n\nSimulation from position j with initial mask M:\n```\nmask = M\nparts_added = 0  # cuts made after starting\nfor pos in range(j, n):\n    b = 1 << (ord(s[pos]) - ord('a'))\n    if mask & b or bin(mask).count('1') < k:\n        mask |= b\n    else:\n        parts_added += 1\n        mask = b\nparts_added += 1  # for the last partition\nreturn parts_added\n```\n\nFor each i (0 to n-1), we compute what to do. Let me try to only iterate over meaningful c's:\n- c already in mask (any such): same as A above.\n- c not in mask, |m|<k: any such c. If c ∉ future window, might allow more...\n- c not in mask, |m|=k: any such c not in m; picking c to maximize future.\n\nActually the answer c depends on what follows. So we may need to try 26 options.\n\nLet me try a different approach. Since n=10^4 and we're allowed one change, maybe we can just enumerate positions and choose \"smart\" c options, at most a constant number per position.\n\nFor state (p, m) at position i:\n- Option 1: don't change or set c to something that behaves like \"adding a char in current mask\". Essentially, pick c that doesn't force cut and doesn't add new distinct.\n  - If s[i] is already such (in m), then no change needed.\n  - Else, if |m| < k, we can set c to any char in m. State after: (p, m). This is like \"removing s[i] from the sequence\" for mask purposes.\n- Option 2: force a cut here. Set c to something not in m and |m|=k, then new partition starts. Or if |m|<k, we can't force a cut just by changing this char (it'd just add to mask). Hmm, unless we cause cut later differently.\n  - When |m|=k and s[i] ∈ m originally (no cut), we could change s[i] to something not in m, forcing a cut. Choose c to be some char not in m — best pick one not likely to appear soon.\n- Option 3: prevent a cut that would naturally happen. If s[i] ∉ m and |m|=k (a natural cut point), change s[i] to something in m. State: (p, m).\n\nThese options give us maybe ~26 possibilities but really the effective set is small.\n\nLet me just code O(n * 26) with a clever simulation.\n\nActually, wait: for each i, the simulation of \"greedy from i+1 with mask M\" can be done, but the same starting position with different M's each take O(n-i) time. Total O(26 * n * n / 2) = 1.3e9. Still slow for Python.\n\nLet me think of precomputation. Precompute for each pos j and each single-char mask {c}: (next cut position, partitions added). But that's still 26n entries and each requires O(n)... hmm no, each takes O(1) amortized if we precompute nextDiff.\n\nActually, here's an idea. Let g(j, M) = number of partitions from greedy starting at j with initial mask M and current partition already having M as its distinct chars.\n\nWe can precompute:\n- next_change[j][M] = position of first cut ≥ j given initial mask M.\n\nBut |M| ≤ k and there are C(26, k) masks. If k is small, feasible. If k=26, only 1 mask matters and everything is 1 partition.\n\nActually for a given starting mask M at position j, if popcount(M) == k, the cut happens at first j' ≥ j with s[j'] ∉ M. If popcount(M) < k, the cut happens when the (k+1)th distinct char is encountered.\n\nLet's just implement the O(n^2 * 26) but efficiently and hope it passes, or use O(n * 26) with smart tricks.\n\nActually let's re-examine. n = 10^4. O(n^2) = 10^8. In Python, 10^8 simple operations is ~100 seconds.",
