      "generated_answer": "<think>\nWe need to try all fillings. N up to 5. Each row has A,B,C placed in 3 of N positions, rest empty. Number of ways per row: C(N,3)*6. For N=5: 10*6=60. Total 60^5 = 7.7e8, too much maybe but with pruning fine.\n\nBetter: backtrack row by row, checking column constraints as we go.\n\nLet me do backtracking. For each row, generate all valid arrangements (positions for A,B,C) such that leftmost non-empty equals R[i]. Then check column constraints incrementally.\n\nColumn state: track count of A,B,C in each column and whether column has any character yet and what the topmost is.\n\nLet me code it.\n\nFor each row i, enumerate placements: choose positions pa, pb, pc distinct in [0,N). Leftmost among them must have the char = R[i].\n\nThen check: for each column j with a char, update counts (must be ≤1 of each), and if column j previously empty and now has a char at row i, the topmost char must be... wait, the topmost is determined by the first row that puts a char in that column. So we need to track for each column the topmost char (or None). When placing in row i, if column j was empty and now gets char X, that char must equal C[j].\n\nAt the end, each column must have exactly one A, B, C — meaning each column's counts are all 1.\n\nLet me implement.\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    from itertools import permutations\n    N, R, C = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(N)\n    \n    # precompute row options for each row\n    row_options = [[] for _ in range(N)]\n    for i in range(N):\n        for positions in permutations(range(N), 3):\n            # positions[0] for A, [1] for B, [2] for C\n            pa, pb, pc = positions\n            # build row\n            row = ['.']*N\n            row[pa]='A'; row[pb]='B'; row[pc]='C'\n            # find leftmost\n            for j in range(N):\n                if row[j]!='.':\n                    if row[j]==R[i]:\n                        row_options[i].append(row[:])\n                    break\n    \n    grid = [None]*N\n    col_top = [None]*N\n    col_count = [[0,0,0] for _ in range(N)]  # A,B,C\n    \n    def idx(ch): return ord(ch)-ord('A')\n    \n    def backtrack(i):\n        if i==N:\n            # check each column has all three\n            for j in range(N):\n                if col_count[j]!=[1,1,1]:\n                    return False\n            return True\n        for row in row_options[i]:\n            # check feasibility\n            ok=True\n            changes=[]\n            for j in range(N):\n                if row[j]!='.':\n                    k=idx(row[j])\n                    if col_count[j][k]>=1:\n                        ok=False; break\n                    if col_top[j] is None:\n                        if row[j]!=C[j]:\n                            ok=False; break\n                        changes.append((j,k,True))\n                        col_top[j]=row[j]\n                        col_count[j][k]+=1\n                    else:\n                        changes.append((j,k,False))\n                        col_count[j][k]+=1\n            if ok:\n                grid[i]=row\n                if backtrack(i+1):\n                    return True\n            # undo\n            for j,k,was_top in changes:\n                col_count[j][k]-=1\n                if was_top:\n                    col_top[j]=None\n        return False\n    \n    if backtrack(0):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n        for r in grid:\n            print(''.join(r))\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```\n\nWait, in the loop when `ok=False` breaks, we still need to undo the changes made so far. Let me fix.\n\nActually I need to undo whether ok or not (after trying the row). Let me restructure:\n\n```python\nfor row in row_options[i]:\n    ok=True\n    changes=[]\n    for j in range(N):\n        if row[j]!='.':\n            k=idx(row[j])\n            if col_count[j][k]>=1:\n                ok=False; break\n            if col_top[j] is None:\n                if row[j]!=C[j]:\n                    ok=False; break\n                col_top[j]=row[j]\n                col_count[j][k]+=1\n                changes.append((j,k,True))\n            else:\n                col_count[j][k]+=1\n                changes.append((j,k,False))\n    if ok:\n        grid[i]=row\n        if backtrack(i+1):\n            return True\n    # undo\n    for j,k,was_top in changes:\n        col_count[j][k]-=1\n        if was_top:\n            col_top[j]=None\n```\n\nThis handles both cases. Good.\n</think>\n\n```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    from itertools import permutations\n    data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    N = int(data[0]); R = data[1]; C = data[2]\n    \n    row_options = [[] for _ in range(N)]\n    for i in range(N):\n        for positions in permutations(range(N), 3):\n            pa, pb, pc = positions\n            row = ['.']*N\n            row[pa]='A'; row[pb]='B'; row[pc]='C'\n            for j in range(N):\n                if row[j]!='.':\n                    if row[j]==R[i]:\n                        row_options[i].append(row[:])\n                    break\n    \n    grid = [None]*N\n    col_top = [None]*N\n    col_count = [[0,0,0] for _ in range(N)]\n    \n    def idx(ch): return ord(ch)-ord('A')\n    \n    def backtrack(i):\n        if i==N:\n            for j in range(N):\n                if col_count[j]!=[1,1,1]:\n                    return False\n            return True\n        for row in row_options[i]:\n            ok=True\n            changes=[]\n            for j in range(N):\n                if row[j]!='.':\n                    k=idx(row[j])\n                    if col_count[j][k]>=1:\n                        ok=False; break\n                    if col_top[j] is None:\n                        if row[j]!=C[j]:\n                            ok=False; break\n                        col_top[j]=row[j]\n                        col_count[j][k]+=1\n                        changes.append((j,k,True))\n                    else:\n                        col_count[j][k]+=1\n                        changes.append((j,k,False))\n            if ok:\n                grid[i]=row\n                if backtrack(i+1):\n                    return True\n            for j,k,was_top in changes:\n                col_count[j][k]-=1\n                if was_top:\n                    col_top[j]=None\n        return False\n    \n    if backtrack(0):\n        print(\"Yes\")\n        for r in grid:\n            print(''.join(r))\n    else:\n        print(\"No\")\n\nsolve()\n```",
