    "global index": "MMLUPro_philosophy_9637",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: E.F. Schumacher, famous economist, in an article in the Atlantic (April 1979): Fifteen months [after I had advised that rural India should have a technology intermediate between the hoe and the tractor,] an all-India conference on intermediate technology was arranged. (They still didn't like the term [intermediate], so they called it appropriate technology. That's all right: when I come to India and somebody says, \"Oh, Mr. Schumacher, I don't believe in appropriate technology\", I just look him straight in the face and say, \"Oh, that's splendid. Do you believe in inappropriate technology?\")\n\nOptions: \nA. Equivocation\nB. Inconsistency\nC. Appeal to Authority\nD. Prejudicial Language\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The statement uses a rhetorical trick by redefining \"appropriate technology\" in a way that forces a false dichotomy, which is a form of equivocation. Schumacher twists the meaning of \"appropriate\" to imply that rejecting the term means preferring \"inappropriate\" technology, rather than understanding the term's specific context. This misuse of ambiguous language fits the fallacy of equivocation.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
