      "generated_answer": "We need the probability that the set of blue vertices is a rotation of the set of red vertices.  \nFor a given coloring, let \\(B\\) be the set of blue vertices. The condition is that there exists a rotation \\(r\\) such that \\(r(B) = B^c\\) (the complement). This implies \\(|B| = 4\\) because the octagon has 8 vertices and a rotation mapping \\(B\\) to its complement forces equal size.  \n\nFor a fixed 4-subset \\(B\\), there is exactly one rotation (by 180°) that could map it to its complement, and this happens iff \\(B\\) is exactly half a rotation away from its complement, i.e., \\(B\\) is a union of 4 antipodal pairs. There are \\(\\binom{4}{2} \\cdot 2^2 = 6 \\cdot 4 = 24\\) such sets? Actually, choose 2 of the 4 antipodal pairs to be blue: \\(\\binom{4}{2}=6\\) ways, and for each chosen pair, both vertices are blue, so 6 sets.  \n\nTotal colorings: \\(2^8 = 256\\). Favorable: 6 sets for blue (and red is complement), so probability = \\(6/256 = 3/128\\).  \nThus \\(m+n = 3+128 = 131\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{131}\n\\]",
